    {
      "filename": "A Journey Over the Pacific. (32298914728).jpg",
      "title": "File:A Journey Over the Pacific. (32298914728).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/81/A_Journey_Over_the_Pacific._%2832298914728%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Journey_Over_the_Pacific._(32298914728).jpg",
      "width": 1280,
      "height": 720,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0",
      "artist": "Ryan  Hallock from Topanga, California, USA",
      "description": "I again apologize for any downgraded quality, I’m still without my main editing laptop, so I’m uploading via a saved version on my iPhone X. \nThis was my first journey out onto the Pacific Ocean to look for whales as part of an Airbnb Experience. \nUnlike most Whale watching boats that hold 45-60 people on a large vessel, and adhering to time restraints, this one was a newer fishing boat. It held a max of 6 people, had a small upper deck which held the captain along with a steering wheel and GPS systems, and a lower deck for sleeping/eating. The owner/captain turned and I ended up forming a small friendship. I spend lots of time off grid, as does he, however he has much more experience in much more wilderness than I. It was an absolutely amazing opportunity. He ended up inviting me to stay on the boat for the afternoon session, which I graciously accepted. \nIt was my first time seeing a whale in its natural habitat, well, not really just one, more like almost 20! Truly a moving experience, one I’ll never ever forget.\n\nShot with a DJI Phantom 4 Pro off the coast of Catalina Island.",
      "dateCreated": "2017-12-08 16:19",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "santa-catalina-island-california-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "9mgv"
    },
